{
  "dom_id": "step-q114811",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/68ccae6ffef49cdd9704deecf4c0c33e025b807c5b5db167825387d720e554ba.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is given above. Find all the values of $x$ where the function $h(x)$ has a relative maximum. You may assume that all of the $x$-intercepts of $y={h}^{′}(x)$ are shown in the picture.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or ${h}^{′}(x)$ is undefined, then we have a critical point.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)>0,$ then $h(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If ${h}^{′}(x)<0,$ then $h(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nThe function $h(x)$ has a relative maximum at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ or it's undefined, and\n\n- ${h}^{′}(x)$ changes its sign from positive to  negative (going from left to right) at that point.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $-4$ | $(-4,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${h}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $h$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{no extr.}$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n\nIn our case, we obtain a relative maximum at $x=-4$ only.",
